# llama-server policy (E5): security and usage defaults, 2026-10-29.
# Owner: E5. Performance lives in the E4 profile (runtime/llama-server.args), which
# this file does not repeat. Requirements: docs/stages/E5-requirements.md.
#
# Format: same as the profile (one flag per line; flag and value separated by one
# space, no quotes and no space in the value; comment right above). The wrapper
# (runtime/llama-server-start.sh) substitutes in the values: ${LLAMA_SERVER_HOST} and
# ${LLAMA_SERVER_PORT} (versions.env) and ${LLM_API_KEY_FILE}
# (default ~/.config/local-agentic/llm-api-key).
#
# Final line: -m <model from models.tsv> + profile + this policy. This file only carries
# what the profile does not have (today no flag is in both).
# Overlap, if it ever happens: in b11982 the last occurrence of each flag wins
# (common/arg.cpp), with the warning "DEPRECATED: argument specified multiple times". So
# the wrapper removes from the profile every flag that is also here, aliases included
# (-n = --predict): THE POLICY WINS, and the final line has no repetition. A different
# value produces a note in the log.
# Host, port, key, CORS and network (POLICY_ONLY list in runtime/llama-server-args.py)
# must not appear in the profile: the wrapper refuses to start.
#
# OFF ON PURPOSE. The E7 container's forwarder exposes every server route to the
# agents, so nothing below may be turned on. All of them are already off in
# b11982; the wrapper refuses to start if any appears in the profile or here (FORBIDDEN list),
# and `scripts/llm health` checks against the running server (POST /props and POST /slots
# answer 501, /tools and /cors-proxy answer 403).
#   --props             POST /props would change global properties at runtime
#   --slot-save-path    POST /slots/<id>?action=save|restore would write and read the cache on disk
#   -ag/--agent, --tools, --tools-runtime, --mcp-servers-config, --mcp-servers-json,
#   --ui-mcp-proxy      built-in tools (shell, files), MCP and the /cors-proxy proxy
#   --media-path, --path  reading local files via file:// and static files
#   --connect           peer-to-peer tunnel out of the machine
#   --log-prompts-dir   would write the prompts (project code) to disk
#   --api-key           key on the command line (visible in ps) and in git
#   -hf, -mu, -dr, --models-dir, --models-preset   downloads and router mode
#   --cors-credentials, --reuse-port

# Loopback interface only (127.0.0.1, from versions.env). WSL forwards localhost
# to Windows, so the browser there can reach the server: hence the key and the CORS below.
--host ${LLAMA_SERVER_HOST}
# Port from versions.env (9931; 8080 belongs to another local service).
--port ${LLAMA_SERVER_PORT}
# Key required on every route except /health and /v1/health (the web UI loads,
# but asks for the key). File outside git, mode 0600, created by `scripts/llm key`.
# Accepts "Authorization: Bearer <key>" (OpenAI) and "x-api-key: <key>" (Anthropic).
--api-key-file ${LLM_API_KEY_FILE}
# CORS for the own origin only: a page from another origin open in the browser cannot read
# the responses. In b11982 the value goes literally into Access-Control-Allow-Origin (the
# "comma-separated list" in the help is not handled), so it is a single origin; the
# special value "localhost" would accept any localhost port. The main barrier is the key.
--cors-origins http://${LLAMA_SERVER_HOST}:${LLAMA_SERVER_PORT}
# No browser credentials in CORS (the b11982 default is on).
--no-cors-credentials
# Built-in tools and CORS proxy off (already the default). Explicit and after
# the profile: empties --tools and turns off --ui-mcp-proxy even if something turned them on.
--no-agent
# No network access to download models: the model comes only from disk (models.tsv).
--offline
#
# Already in the E4 profile and not repeated here: --metrics (/metrics, requires the key),
# --temp 0.6, --top-p 0.95 and --top-k 20 (model card). The wrapper requires all
# four to be in the final line (profile or policy) and refuses to start without them.
#
# Sampling from the model card that the profile lacks. Applies to clients that do not send the
# parameters (Codex sends none); each request can override. min-p 0 (llama.cpp default: 0.05).
--min-p 0
# Same: presence penalty 0 (already the default; explicit because it is in the model card).
--presence-penalty 0
# Default output limit per request: Codex does not send max_output_tokens, and long
# tool arguments must not be cut off. It is not a ceiling: in b11982 the request's max_tokens
# wins (server-context.cpp, "the per-request limit takes priority").
-n 32768
